  "body": "Testcase like so:\r\n\r\n    <body>\r\n      <script>\r\n      var el = document.createElement(\"test\");\r\n      var b = document.body;\r\n      document.replaceChild(el, document.documentElement);\r\n      try {\r\n        document.body = b;\r\n        alert(\"New body: \" + document.body);\r\n      } catch (e) {\r\n        alert(e);\r\n      }\r\n      </script>\r\n    </body>\r\n\r\nObserved UA behavior:\r\n\r\n* Firefox: Throws on the `document .body` assignment\r\n* Edge: Alerts `\"[object HTMLBodyElement]\"`\r\n* Chrome/Safari: Alert null\r\n\r\nSpec says to alert null, because https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#dom-document-body setter steps step 5 doesn't worry about whether \"the document element\" is \"the html element\", while the getter, via https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#the-body-element-2 does worry about it.  So it will let you assign document.body and then echo back null.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure I'm totally happy with any of the behaviors, and I'm probably OK with the as-is spec behavior if it's actually intentional, as opposed to just an oversight.",
